<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ryukyu Islands</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nansei Islands</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ryukyu Islands are a chain of islands stretching south-west from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>, of which Okinawa is the largest. They were the home of the Ryukyu Kingdom, a trading state that paid tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> annexed them in 1879. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-okinawa">Battle of Okinawa</a> in 1945 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> governed them until their return to Japan in 1972.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ryukyu-islands.jpg" alt="Ryukyu Islands" width="330" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 浅見真規 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Test_20121016_1445.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The chain is also called the Nansei Islands, literally the southwest islands, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> has used that name on nautical charts since 1907. Administratively it divides between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagoshima-prefecture">Kagoshima Prefecture</a>, whose Satsunan Islands include the Ōsumi, Tokara and Amami groups, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okinawa-prefecture">Okinawa Prefecture</a>, which holds the Okinawa, Miyako, Yaeyama and Daitō Islands. The larger islands are mostly volcanic and the smaller mostly coral. The climate runs from humid subtropical in the north to tropical rainforest in the south. The Watase Line marks a major biogeographic boundary, with the Ōsumi Islands in the Palearctic realm and the Amami Islands at the northern limit of the Indomalayan realm.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Chinese sources mention eastern islands of immortals, and in 601 and 607 Chinese expeditions visited the Country of Liuqiu. Japanese records list the Southern Islands from 616, and in 997 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> identified as Amami islanders raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a>. Tanegashima passed into the Shimazu Estate in the 12th century, and around 1543 a Chinese junk carrying Portuguese merchants was driven there; Tanegashima Tokitaka reproduced their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matchlock">matchlock</a> rifles, which spread across Sengoku Japan as tanegashima.</p><p>Polities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okinawa-island">Okinawa Islands</a> were unified as the Ryukyu Kingdom in 1429, a tributary state of Ming China, which conquered the Miyako and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaeyama-islands">Yaeyama Islands</a> and at its peak ruled Amami. The Amami Islands were disputed between the kingdom and the southward-expanding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma Domain</a> until Satsuma conquered them in the 1609 invasion of the kingdom, with a fleet of 13 junks and 2,500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a>. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sho-nei">Shō Nei</a> ordered surrender to spare lives. The kingdom then paid tribute to both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> and the Chinese emperor, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> granted Satsuma the Amami islands in 1624. The Meiji government annexed the kingdom in 1879 and suppressed Ryukyuan identity, tradition and language.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Amami, Okinawa, Miyako and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaeyama-islands">Yaeyama Islands</a> have a native population called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryukyuans">Ryukyuan people</a>, who traditionally speak several Ryukyuan languages, though Japanese is now the primary language. Their religion centres on ancestor respect and the relations among the living, the dead and the spirits of nature. The islands were held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-san-francisco">Treaty of San Francisco</a> of 1951 and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> under the 1971 Okinawa reversion agreement. Yakushima, with its ancient cedars, was designated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1993.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ryukyu Islands” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ships from China, the <b>Ryukyu Islands</b>, Java and the Maluku Islands came in on the northeast monsoon from December to January, and ships for the Indian coasts, the Red Sea and East Africa left on the southwest monsoon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malacca-sultanate">Malacca Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Irrigated wet-rice farming itself came from the Yangtze estuary in southern China, reaching Japan through the <b>Ryukyu Islands</b> or the Korean Peninsula.</blockquote><a href="/culture/yayoi-culture">Yayoi culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Japan’s expedition to Taiwan in 1874 and its annexation of the <b>Ryukyu Islands</b> in 1879 revealed a new danger close to home.</blockquote><a href="/movement/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Japan, under the motto “Enrich the state, strengthen the military”, annexed the <b>Ryukyu Islands</b> in 1879, took Taiwan after the First Sino-Japanese War, annexed Korea in 1910, took Manchuria in 1931 and by 1942 had conquered much of East Asia and the Pacific.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/imperialism">Imperialism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1950 it was replaced by the United States Civil Administration of the <b>Ryukyu Islands</b>, and the San Francisco treaty of 1951 left the islands under American control.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-states-civil-administration-of-the-ryukyu-islands">United States Civil Administration of the Ryukyu Islands</a></li></ul></section>
